          "id": "4ca3ec3bd14537301dccb915f88fa70020e29eaa",
          "message": "refactor(pixelflow-codegen): give the assembler an operand vocabulary (#1059)\n\n## The rule\n\n> Literal machine code belongs only in an assembler — a function that\ntranslates one op into its bytes.\n\nThe four `IsaBackend` drivers held **51 sites** that broke it:\n\n```rust\ncode.extend_from_slice(&[0x49, 0x89, 0xCA]); // mov r10, rcx\n```\n\nThe mnemonic lived in a comment, which nothing checks. All 51 are now\ncalls to named instructions. **Every driver is at zero raw opcode\nsites.**\n\n## Operands are parameters, not part of the name\n\n`mov_r64_r64` would put the operand kinds back into the name — the same\nnamespace-inside-a-name smell as the `avx2_slot_disp` /\n`avx512_frame_bytes` family retired in #1055. The operand *types* select\nthe encoding instead:\n\n```rust\nadd(code, RSI, R8)           // REX.W 01 /r\nadd(code, RSI, Imm8(16))     // REX.W 83 /0 ib\nadd(code, RSP, Imm32(n))     // REX.W 81 /0 id\nadd(code, X1, X1, Imm12(1))  // aarch64 immediate form\nadd(code, X1, X1, X4)        // aarch64 register form\n```\n\nOne mnemonic, one name. A caller never picks an encoding — which is the\nentire job of the layer.\n\n## That required the register files to become types\n\nThey weren't. `Reg` named the **vector** file; general registers had no\ntype at all — bare `u8` in a couple of encoder signatures and raw bytes\neverywhere else. Both conventions collided in one existing call:\n\n```rust\nemit_umov_w(c, 10, Reg(28), 0)\n//             ^^ GPR as a bare u8      ^^^^^^^ vector register as Reg\n```\n\n`Gpr` and `Xr` now name the general files. `Gpr(10)` is `r10`, `Reg(10)`\nis `xmm10`, and neither can be passed where the other belongs.\n\n## Nine instructions, and seven\n\nThat the vocabulary is this small is the point — a SIMD language barely\ntouches general registers. They appear only as loop counters, the output\npointer, and the row stride.\n\n| | ops |\n|---|---|\n| x86-64 | `mov` `xor` `cmp` `inc` `add` `sub` `jae` `jmp` `ret` |\n| aarch64 | `movz` `cmp` `add` `str_q` `b` `b_hs` `ret` `mvn_w` |\n\n**Masks needed no third file.** Hardware has one on AVX-512 (k0–k7) and\nnone on NEON, but a mask *value* is an ordinary all-ones/all-zeros\nvector on every target here — `vcmpps` writes `k1` and `vpmovm2d`\nimmediately widens it back into an allocatable zmm. So `k1` is fixed\nscratch inside three encoders and never an operand.\n\n## De-triplication\n\n`sub rsp, imm32`, `add rsp, imm32` and `ret` had a copy in each of the\nthree x86 files. They're general-register instructions, identical at\nevery vector width, and are now defined once.\n\n## Verification\n\nEvery encoder is unit-tested against the manual's form for that\nmnemonic, using **the exact bytes the drivers previously spelled\ninline** — which is what makes the replacement provably byte-identical.\n\nAnd it was verified as such: the emitted collapse-loop scaffold for\n**all four backends** is byte-for-byte unchanged, captured before and\nafter. That check only exists because #1056 made every backend compile\non every host — an x86 box can emit and compare NEON.\n\n`cargo fmt --check`; clippy `--all-targets` clean on x86-64 and\n`aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu`; aarch64 lib codegen (which is what accepts\nthe encodings); full tests at SSE2, AVX2+FMA and AVX-512F+DQ; workspace\ngreen.\n\nOne thing worth noting for review: the per-ISA test modules only compile\nat their own `target_feature`, so a break in them is invisible to the\ndefault build. That bit me mid-change and was caught by running all\nthree levels locally — the same blind spot this series keeps finding,\none layer down.\n\n## Not in this PR\n\nThe four `emit_collapse_loop` bodies are still four (~390 lines of\nnear-identical loop nest). Now that they're written in named ops rather\nthan bytes, unifying them behind semantic verbs on `IsaBackend` is a\nmuch smaller and more readable change than it would have been before.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude\nCode](https://claude.ai/code/session_01Eji8JfyLCcTRmbbhyQCvWV)_\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>",
